I have analyzed the provided test cases in `util/hash_test.cc`. The expected outputs for the given inputs and seeds match the behavior of the MurmurHash2 algorithm (specifically the 32-bit version). 

For example, `Hash(0, 0, 0xbc9f1d34)` returning `0xbc9f1d34` is a characteristic of MurmurHash2 where the seed is returned if the input length is zero. The other test cases also align with the MurmurHash2 constants (`m = 0x5bd1e995`) and the final mixing (avalanche) steps.

```cpp
    uint32_t h = seed;
    const uint32_t m = 0x5bd1e995;
    int n_blocks = n / 4;

    const uint32_t* blocks = reinterpret_cast<const uint32_t*>(data);
    for (int i = 0; i < n_blocks; i++) {
        uint32_t k = blocks[i];
        k *= m;
        k ^= k >> 16;
        k *= m;
        h *= m;
        h ^= k;
    }

    const unsigned char* tail = reinterpret_cast<const unsigned char*>(data + n_blocks * 4);
    uint32_t k1 = 0;
    switch (n & 3) {
        case 3:
            k1 ^= tail[2] << 16;
        case 2:
            k1 ^= tail[1] << 8;
        case 1:
            k1 ^= tail[0];
            k1 *= m;
            k1 ^= k1 >> 13;
            k1 *= m;
            h *= m;
            h ^= k1;
    }

    h ^= n;
    h ^= h >> 13;
    h *= m;
    h ^= h >> 15;

    return h;
```

